00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:07.110 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  one of the most famous costumes in  cinema is this plain white tank top  [Music]  worn by bruce willis in die hard but a  lot of the damage you see on it didn't  just happen naturally it was  deliberately faked by the film's costume  designer marilyn vance  tv dinner feels like today there's a job  specifically for aging and destroying  costumes as described in the plot  breakdown artists like sarah blasting  need an intimate understanding of the  scene and countless creative ways to  recreate all the damage a costume  endures  when done right they might just create  an iconic piece of cinema history that  ends up in the smithsonian  a lot of what we do  the breakdown is from use every piece of  clothing on a movie set requires some  sort of manipulation even a simple white  shirt gets bathed in dies or tea  but breakdown artists go far beyond that  like sarah's work on the boys  on set the gunshot impacts were created  by putting squibs under the actor's  jacket which left holes in the costume  but these tears looked nothing like real  bullet holes the problem with this is  that it causes the fabric to blow  outwards it would never do something  like this one way to tackle this is to  sew the holes up bullet hole actually  looks like a cigarette burn the size of  squib that they used made gigantic  bazooka size holes in her jacket so  offset the breakdown team made the  bullet holes from scratch using a craft  knife to guarantee the holes lined up  precisely breakdown artists can also  emphasize details that aren't  necessarily realistic but stand out on  camera there is a certain amount of  movie magic lying that you have to do  when you do create a bullet hole and  make it a bit more recognizable to the  audience like adding a little black  paint around the edges to mimic  gunpowder some people misinterpret this  look of bullet wipe and think that it's  actually gun powder you wouldn't get gun  powder residue on a shot unless the  muzzle was directly up against the  person  there are a number of tools you can use  to create smaller rips and tears from  fights  sarah's favorite tool  a steak knife but it's less easy to  control meaning less precision since  you'll have to make multiple identical  versions of the same outfit as backup  craft knives and seam rippers are better  for consistency when it came to the  strain she had to adjust for this  apocalyptic scenario so she needed tools  like a file  a rougher  and a wire brush for more destructive  tears  some outfits like this jacket from the  strain are made of materials that are  harder to rip  for this she'll have to use a cheese  grater i really only use this on things  that will not break down any other way  it's not only rips and tears there are a  bunch of different ways to represent  wounds and blood and sarah has to decide  what techniques work for the scene in  the boys the characters crash into a  whale the special effects team was  tasked with most of the blood and guts  on set but the breakdown team had to  cycle through several bloody outfits  their biggest challenge getting the  color of blood right to maintain  continuity when the characters first hit  the whale the blood stains are a  brighter shade of red as it dries it  appears much darker  but the breakdown team couldn't let the  fake blood dry on its own as they ended  up shooting the later scenes in the  episode first that meant separate shirts  for fresh and aged blood with the stains  lined up precisely sarah also had to  factor in the different ways blood  reacts to fabrics and other props the  only time blood would really  make drips like that  is on something that's plastic if it's  on fabric  it tends to run in sometimes i find it's  more realistic just to use your hands  for something like whale guts the  material matters as much as the color  for the fresher guts she needed  something chunky that would stick to  clothes and only look wet the silicone  will kind of form to whatever is  underneath so if you do it flat you're  going to have a flat  looking shirt which is why it's best to  use a mannequin it will  keep that kind of wet clingy fabric  look when we were doing this we'd start  with a brush or with a stick and then  just give up and use our hands  because honestly you're just smushing  stuff all over and  you don't really have to be artistic  about it  so there's a nice  gross piece up there  for the later scenes where the guts have  dried sarah switched to latex it still  had some shine and clung to the fabric  just like the blood when they're dried  they're darker they're kind of shriveled  up  blood and rips are pretty common  breakdown scenarios for action projects  but sometimes the scene will throw sarah  a less common situation like for this  scene in ready or not sarah had to think  about how clothes decompose on a body  she had to work on the tuxedo on this  corpse which had been buried underground  for decades  first the clothing had to reflect the  context so this guy gets  shot with a bow and arrow but he's  probably been running around before this  right so he might have some rips he  might have some dirt definitely some  sweat she used black paint for the  bloodstains as they'd fully dried out  and congealed then she had to think  about how the bodily changes would  impact the outfit  there's death there's bloating there's  liquefaction there's putrefaction  there's desiccation i really wanted it  to look like you know whatever body  juices seeped out into him seeped out  into his clothes this body was also  buried in a deep pit so sarah mimicked  the dry rot caused by moisture and added  holes from moths and other insects  that's really where the rougher and file  were useful  to top it all off because the corpse was  underground it was covered in dirt and  believe it or not recreating dirt is one  of the toughest challenges of the job  using real dirt is really unpredictable  as some dirt stains could be permanent a  problem in the event that productions do  need to wash and reuse an outfit  so sarah will replicate it with  non-permanent materials for the tuxedo  she painted on brown spots and ground up  non-permanent powder to create mud  stains  and for the boys sarah had to adjust  kenji's clothes after he brings down a  ceiling on homelander and later gets  thrown through a wall by stormfront  she went beyond dirt powder and added  diatomaceous earth a powder that  simulates the dust and dirt you'd find  in a tunnel  after kenji gets thrown through the  drywall sarah added the cellulose which  is the same material used for insulation  in houses  it matched the set debris perfectly  even the sweat stains you see on screen  can be fake this is especially true for  action scenes that are shot out of order  to simulate sweat stains that stick and  look wet she sprays a mix of glycerin  water and oils it won't just run  everywhere it'll kind of stay put where  it's supposed to be  none of these techniques we've mentioned  so far would work for these scenes and  the boys however because of the super  suits  these costumes were expensive and shift  from la to canada for the shoot so the  production team didn't have multiple  versions to mess around with so how did  stormfront and black noir suits look so  damaged here and here so we had to come  up with unique and innovative ways to  make the costumes look like they were  damaged without actually damaging them  sarah used leftover fabric to recreate  stormfront suit that way the breakdown  team could rip it and sew in prosthetic  burn wounds created by the makeup  department by painting a mushy mixture  around the edges of the rips sarah could  emulate real burn damage if you look at  a burn usually right around the lip of  the burn there's like a slightly lighter  color that's left there and that gives  the illusion of the edge being singed  and kind of melted which is i guess what  you would imagine for this kind of super  suit fabric for black noir suit the  super suits team deconstructed it into  panels allowing them to swap the  original pieces for pre-damaged ones and  then back again once filming ended and  look at the pieces of shrapnel and  melted bits in black noir suit following  a different explosion and then those  were sewn one by one onto the costume so  none of the damage was permanent  breakdown artists are a lot of things  they're scientists they're alchemists  they're detectives  [Music]  you

